# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ARES-1 landing — redirects and hard 404s.
# Checklist §2.2 (nothing sensitive is served), §8.3 (a real 404 page).
#
# Format: `_redirects` — understood by Cloudflare Pages AND by Netlify. This is
# the portable counterpart of the `[[redirects]]` blocks that used to live in
# netlify.toml; Cloudflare Pages ignores netlify.toml entirely, so rules kept
# only there were dead.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: the first matching rule wins. The hard 404s must stay above the
# SPA catch-all, otherwise the catch-all returns 200 + index.html for
# /.git/HEAD and a scanner sees a live path that does not exist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Paths that must never resolve (§2.2).
# These are not the protection — the protection is that only dist/ is
# published — but they give an honest 404 and a clean signal in
# scripts/check-public-exposure.sh.
/.git/*              /404.html   404
/.env*               /404.html   404
/*.map               /404.html   404
/package.json        /404.html   404
/package-lock.json   /404.html   404
/yarn.lock           /404.html   404
/.npmrc              /404.html   404
/.github/*           /404.html   404
/docs/*              /404.html   404
/scripts/*           /404.html   404
/game/*              /404.html   404
/watchtower/*        /404.html   404

# SPA fallback. Every remaining unknown path serves the shell — which is exactly
# why the exposure check compares response CONTENT and not just the status code.
/*                   /index.html 200
